From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Builder <builder@example.invalid>
Date: Mon, 25 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] usb: phy_meson_usb: log U2P_R0 readback around the phy reset

Diagnostic only. No register is written and no control flow changes. This is
deliberately a separate patch from 0010 so that one build carries exactly one
behavioural change and one set of extra prints, and the two can be read apart.

The question it answers is whether the U2P_R0 write that 0009 adds actually
lands, and whether it survives the phy config reset pulse that follows it.
Nothing else on this image can answer that:

- CONFIG_DEVMEM is not set, so there is no /dev/mem.
- Busybox carries no devmem applet, and no package can add one here.
- The driver exposes no debugfs or sysfs register view -- there is no
  debugfs, DEVICE_ATTR or sysfs_create anywhere in phy_meson_usb/.
- A warm reboot to the U-Boot prompt resets the phy block. Two registers in
  it were measured coming back as their handover values, pll_cfg 0x80549540
  and trim 0x0000007f, rather than whatever the kernel had left. So the
  bootloader prompt cannot report post-kernel state for this block, which is
  a structural limit and not a procedural one.

That leaves a print from inside the driver as the only route.

Two prints, both on the path the failing boot actually takes.

The first is a promotion, not an addition. meson_u2phy_aml_init() already
reads r0/r1/r2 twice, but both readbacks are dev_dbg. With
CONFIG_DYNAMIC_DEBUG unset, no DEBUG define in this directory, and
DYNAMIC_DEBUG_MODULE defined nowhere in the tree -- it appears in no Makefile
and in no kbuild script -- dev_dbg resolves to dev_no_printk, whose argument
list sits inside an if (0). So today those readl calls do not execute and
nothing is printed. CONFIG_DYNAMIC_DEBUG_CORE=y does not change that: the
header wants DYNAMIC_DEBUG_MODULE alongside it.

Promoting the second one to dev_info makes it real. It sits before 0009's
write, so it is the baseline the write is supposed to change.

The other existing readback, the one labelled "end r0~r2", is deliberately
left alone: it is unreachable on the boot under investigation. The lines
above it are

    ret = priv->set_pll(phy);
    if (ret)
            return ret;

and set_pll is exactly what returns -110 here, so the function returns before
reaching it. Promoting that one would print nothing on the failing boot.

The second print is new, and it is the one that answers the question. It
reads r0 and r1 after meson_u2phy_reset_phycfg() and its settling delay --
after 0009's write and after the reset that might clobber it. U2P_R1 bit 0 is
phy_rdy, so it also shows the ready bit at that instant, at no cost.

It is placed before the ready poll. The POR release that 0010 adds is after
that poll, so this readback reports the same state whether or not 0010 is
applied, and a log from either build is comparable with a log from the other.
The consequence is worth stating plainly: this patch measures 0009's write,
not 0010's. Reading back the POR release would need a third print and would
put two variables in one log.

Not free of effect. A dev_info on a serial console costs milliseconds, and
these two land before priv->cali(), before the ready poll and before the PLL
loop, so the timing of everything downstream shifts. That is inherent to any
printk-based readback here and there is no lower-cost route on this image; it
is recorded so a changed outcome is not mistaken for the readback being
passive.

Reading the result: if the post-reset r0 shows POR, host_device, IDPULLUP0
and DRVVBUS0 set and the boot still fails, the register state is right and
the cause is elsewhere. If it does not, 0009 never did what it claimed and
0010 is stacked on nothing. Either answer is progress; the present state,
where neither is known, is not.
---
--- a/common_drivers/drivers/usb/phy_meson_usb/phy-meson-aml-usb2-com.c
+++ b/common_drivers/drivers/usb/phy_meson_usb/phy-meson-aml-usb2-com.c
@@ -808,7 +808,14 @@
 	usleep_range(50, 100);
 	//meson_u2phy_reset_comb(phy);
 
-	dev_dbg(phy->dev, "init r0~r2 0x%x 0x%x 0x%x.\n",
+	/*
+	 * Promoted from dev_dbg. With CONFIG_DYNAMIC_DEBUG unset and no
+	 * DEBUG or DYNAMIC_DEBUG_MODULE define in this directory, every
+	 * dev_dbg() here resolves to dev_no_printk() -- the readl() calls
+	 * sit in dead code and nothing is printed. This is the baseline
+	 * state, read before the U2P_R0 write that 0009 adds below.
+	 */
+	dev_info(phy->dev, "init r0~r2 0x%x 0x%x 0x%x.\n",
 			readl(&phy->u2p_aml_regs[0]->r0),
 			readl(&phy->u2p_aml_regs[0]->r1),
 			readl(&phy->u2p_aml_regs[0]->r2));
@@ -846,6 +853,32 @@
 	meson_u2phy_reset_phycfg(phy);
 	usleep_range(50, 100);
 
+	/*
+	 * The measurement this patch exists for. This is after the U2P_R0
+	 * write 0009 adds and after the phy config reset pulse that might
+	 * clobber it, so one line answers two questions: did the write
+	 * land, and does it survive the reset.
+	 *
+	 * It cannot be read any other way on this build. CONFIG_DEVMEM is
+	 * unset so there is no /dev/mem, busybox carries no devmem applet,
+	 * this driver exposes no debugfs or sysfs register view, and a
+	 * warm reboot into U-Boot resets the phy block -- two registers in
+	 * it were measured returning to their handover values rather than
+	 * their post-kernel ones, so the U-Boot prompt cannot report what
+	 * the kernel left behind.
+	 *
+	 * Placed before the ready poll, so it reads the same state whether
+	 * or not the POR release that 0010 adds after that poll is
+	 * present. r1 bit 0 is phy_rdy, free at the same instant.
+	 *
+	 * Diagnostic only: no register is written here. It is not free of
+	 * effect, though -- a dev_info() on a serial console costs
+	 * milliseconds, which delays priv->cali() and everything after it.
+	 */
+	dev_info(phy->dev, "post-phycfg-reset r0~r1 0x%x 0x%x.\n",
+			readl(&phy->u2p_aml_regs[0]->r0),
+			readl(&phy->u2p_aml_regs[0]->r1));
+
 	priv->cali(phy);
 
 	ret = meson_usb2phy_wait_ready(phy, 200);
-- 
2.43.0
